; The scheduler's timer, timer 1 (QUUX's timer-driven scheduler, TS-3 and
; TS-4): BEG06 turns it on, one-shot, with its interrupt enable, at period 0
; (sys/ucadr/uc-cold-disk.lisp), and INTR-TIMER-1 takes its rise: word 112
; written with 403 (the flag cleared, the timer left on and one-shot), and a
; sequence break asked for while :clock is enabled
; (sys/ucadr/uc-interrupt.lisp).  Load lisp/scheduler-timer.lisp first; run
; it with --network files, so that no Ethernet frame gives a pass
; (README.md).  Microcode before it leaves timer 1 off, so 112 reads 0 and
; the first pass after the write is the clock's, about 0.9 s later.
;
; After the boot, 112 reads 405 (octal, as every value of word 112 here):
; on, one-shot, interrupt enable.
(st-control) => "405"
; Its period is 0, and the counting process sees passes.
(st-word #o113) => 0
(st-setup) => T
; A period of 5,000 us written while the listener computes: a pass within
; 10 ms of the write, the loop not starved after the rise (a flag never
; cleared would interrupt for ever), and 112 back at 405, twice.
(st-pass-check 5000 10000) => (T T "405")
(st-pass-check 5000 10000) => (T T "405")
; The latency of the last, in us, and the loop's rounds in 50 ms before the
; write and after the rise.
*st-last*
; With sequence breaks off, (sb-on nil), the rise asks for none: no pass in
; 300 ms, the loop not starved, 112 at 405.
(st-sb-off-check 5000) => (NIL T "405")
*st-last*
; Breaks back on: the timer serves again.
(st-pass-check 2000 10000) => (T T "405")
